1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:07,462
Chapter 58: Hepatic, Biliary, and Pancreatic Disorders.

3
00:00:07,462 --> 00:00:20,337
Hepatic Disorders Gallbladder Disorders Pancreatic Disorders References Disorders of the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas together constitute a formidable list of complications that may arise in pregnancy.

4
00:00:20,337 --> 00:00:25,512
Some stem from preexisting conditions, and some are unique to gestation.

5
00:00:25,512 --> 00:00:30,712
The relationships of several of these with pregnancy can be intriguing and challenging.

6
00:00:30,712 --> 00:00:32,837
Hepatic Disorders.

7
00:00:32,837 --> 00:00:38,212
Liver diseases complicating pregnancy are placed into three general categories.

8
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:44,775
The first includes those specific to pregnancy and resolve either spontaneously or following delivery.

9
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:58,837
Hyperemesis gravidarum, intrahepatic cholestasis, acute fatty liver, and HELLP syndrome—which is characterized by hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, and low platelet counts, are examples.

10
00:00:58,837 --> 00:01:06,787
The second category involves acute hepatic disorders that are coincidental to pregnancy, such as acute viral hepatitis.

11
00:01:06,787 --> 00:01:16,587
Last are chronic liver diseases that predate pregnancy, such as chronic viral or autoimmune hepatitis, cirrhosis, or esophageal varices.

12
00:01:16,587 --> 00:01:21,050
Laboratory testing can aid differentiation of these disorders.

13
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:29,612
Hepatic dysfunction from hyperemesis gravidarum may manifest as mildly elevated serum bilirubin and transaminase levels .

14
00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:32,987
Others listed in Table 58-1.

15
00:01:32,987 --> 00:01:35,562
can show more marked findings.

16
00:01:35,562 --> 00:01:48,100
One example, severe preeclampsia syndrome, discussed in Chapter 40 (p. 689), can be further complicated by the HELLP syndrome, which can rarely cause liver failure .

17
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,625
TABLE 58-1.

18
00:01:50,625 --> 00:02:07,387
Clinical Findings with Liver Diseases in Pregnancy Importantly, several normal pregnancy-induced physiological changes induce appreciable liver-related clinical and laboratory manifestations (Chap. 4, p. 70 and Appendix, p. 1229).

19
00:02:07,387 --> 00:02:17,075
Abnormalities such as increased serum alkaline phosphatase levels, palmar erythema, and spider angiomas are common during normal pregnancy.

20
00:02:17,075 --> 00:02:26,212
In addition, higher levels of estrogen, progesterone, and other pregnancy hormones alter expression of the cytochrome P450 system.

21
00:02:26,212 --> 00:02:35,737
For example, hepatic CYP1A2 expression declines, but that of CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 rises.

22
00:02:35,737 --> 00:02:46,112
The latter pair possibly affects metabolism of commonly used therapeutic agents in pregnancy (Dallmann, 2018; Ornoy, 2019).

23
00:02:46,112 --> 00:02:50,050
Cytochrome enzymes are also expressed in the placenta.

24
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:56,162
The net effect is complex and likely influenced by gestational age and organ of expression .

25
00:02:56,162 --> 00:03:03,400
Despite these functional changes, no major hepatic histological changes are induced by normal pregnancy.

26
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,812
# Acute Liver Failure in Pregnancy.

27
00:03:06,812 --> 00:03:11,187
Fortunately, liver failure is uncommon during pregnancy.

28
00:03:11,187 --> 00:03:19,350
Of the various causes, drug-induced liver injury (DILI) is probably the most frequent nonpregnancy-related etiology .

29
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:24,450
Acetaminophen toxicity is the most prevalent cause in the United States .

30
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:38,312
Other sources of liver failure include acute fatty liver of pregnancy (AFLP), fulminant viral hepatitis, environmental toxins, autoimmune hepatitis, shock liver, and alternative medicines.

31
00:03:38,312 --> 00:03:49,087
In a highly selective study of 70 referred women with a hepatic encephalopathy, half were caused by AFLP and half were associated with HELLP syndrome .

32
00:03:49,087 --> 00:03:54,875
The treatment of acute liver failure in pregnancy mirrors that for nonpregnant individuals.

33
00:03:54,875 --> 00:04:06,737
Therapy targets the underlying etiology, and a team of obstetricians, maternal-fetal medicine and critical care specialists, hepatologists, and transplant surgeons is assembled.

34
00:04:06,737 --> 00:04:22,825
Laboratory studies initially include serum levels of transaminases, bilirubin, amylase, lipase, electrolytes, creatinine, albumin, fibrinogen, lactate, and total cholesterol; complete blood count; and coagulation studies .

35
00:04:22,825 --> 00:04:34,975
Etiologytargeted testing is described fully in later sections but broadly includes viral and autoimmune serological assays and testing for acetaminophen, iron, and copper toxicity.

36
00:04:34,975 --> 00:04:42,975
Computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging may be needed to investigate hepatic structure.

37
00:04:42,975 --> 00:04:47,625
For a pregnancyrelated etiology, delivery is undertaken.

38
00:04:47,625 --> 00:04:56,325
Because of comorbid coagulopathy, vaginal delivery is preferable if it can be achieved expeditiously and fetal condition allows.

39
00:04:56,325 --> 00:04:58,412
Hepatotoxic

40
00:04:58,564 --> 00:05:07,564
agents are avoided, coagulopathy is corrected, and monitoring for cerebral edema and increased intracranial pressure is necessary .

41
00:05:07,514 --> 00:05:15,464
Liver transplantation is problematic during pregnancy, but delivery before transplantation is considered if necessary .

42
00:05:15,464 --> 00:05:19,276
# Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy.

43
00:05:19,276 --> 00:05:29,864
Characterized by pruritus, jaundice, or both, this condition has been called recurrent jaundice of pregnancy, cholestatic hepatosis, and icterus gravidarum.

44
00:05:29,864 --> 00:05:39,139
Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) is more common in multifetal pregnancy, and genetic influences are significant .

45
00:05:39,139 --> 00:05:43,101
Because of this, its incidence varies by population.

46
00:05:43,101 --> 00:05:55,326
In North America, the overall incidence approximates 1 case in 500 to 1000 pregnancies, but its rate nears 5.6 percent among pregnant Latina women in Los Angeles .

47
00:05:55,326 --> 00:06:02,401
Historically, indigenous women from Chile and Bolivia also have a relatively high incidence of ICP.

48
00:06:02,401 --> 00:06:09,401
For unknown reasons, this incidence has decreased since the 1970s and is now less than 2 percent .

49
00:06:09,401 --> 00:06:16,701
In Sweden, China, and Israel, the incidence varies from 0.25 to 1.5 percent .

50
00:06:16,701 --> 00:06:24,401
Pathogenesis The cause of ICP is unclear, but changes in various sex steroid hormone levels are implicated.

51
00:06:24,401 --> 00:06:33,089
In one study, women treated with vaginal progesterone for preterm labor prophylaxis showed a fourfold increase in the ICP rate .

52
00:06:33,089 --> 00:06:40,539
Current research focuses on the numerous mutations in the many genes that control hepatocellular transport systems.

53
00:06:40,539 --> 00:06:53,076
One example is mutations of the ABCB4 gene, which encodes multidrug resistance protein 3 (MDR3) associated with progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis.

54
00:06:53,076 --> 00:06:59,114
Another is error in the ABCB11 gene, which encodes a bile-salt export pump .

55
00:06:59,114 --> 00:07:11,339
Other potential gene products are the farnesoid X receptor and transporting ATPase encoded by ATP8B1 (Abu-Hayyeh, 2016; Sticova, 2018).

56
00:07:11,339 --> 00:07:15,189
The latter predisposes to drug-induced cholestasis.

57
00:07:15,189 --> 00:07:23,764
In this regard, we have encountered impressive cholestatic jaundice in gravidas taking azathioprine following renal transplantation.

58
00:07:23,764 --> 00:07:30,251
Following the inciting cause(s), bile acids are incompletely cleared and accumulate in plasma.

59
00:07:30,251 --> 00:07:40,014
Hyperbilirubinemia results from retention of conjugated pigment, but total plasma concentrations rarely exceed 4 to 5 mg/dL.

60
00:07:40,014 --> 00:07:45,889
Alkaline phosphatase levels are usually elevated even more than for normal pregnancy.

61
00:07:45,889 --> 00:07:52,739
Serum transaminase levels are normal to moderately elevated but seldom exceed 200 U/L .

62
00:07:52,739 --> 00:08:00,601
Liver biopsy shows mild cholestasis with bile plugs in the hepatocytes and canaliculi of the centrilobular regions.

63
00:08:00,601 --> 00:08:03,751
Inflammation or necrosis is absent.

64
00:08:03,751 --> 00:08:11,826
These changes disappear after delivery but often recur in subsequent pregnancies or with estrogen-containing contraceptives.

65
00:08:11,826 --> 00:08:20,976
Clinical Presentation Generalized pruritus that shows predilection for the palms and soles usually develops in late pregnancy or occasionally earlier.

66
00:08:20,976 --> 00:08:27,614
Constitutional symptoms are absent, and skin changes are limited to excoriations from scratching.

67
00:08:27,614 --> 00:08:33,676
Serum transaminase and bile acid levels are measured in women with suspected ICP.

68
00:08:33,676 --> 00:08:42,251
As a threshold for comparison, total serum bile acid levels typically remain <10 μmol/L throughout normal pregnancy .

69
00:08:42,251 --> 00:08:49,726
Elevated total serum bile acid or transaminase levels plus pruritus supports an ICP diagnosis.

70
00:08:49,726 --> 00:08:56,739
Biochemical tests may be abnormal at presentation or may follow initial pruritus after several weeks.

71
00:08:56,739 --> 00:09:03,526
Moreover, a rise in transaminase levels may precede an increase in serum bile acid levels .

72
00:09:03,526 --> 00:09:07,739
Approximately 10 percent of women have concurrent jaundice.

73
00:09:07,739 --> 00:09:16,076
With normal liver enzyme levels or with specific skin findings, pruritus may instead reflect other dermatological disorders .

74
00:09:16,076 --> 00:09:23,301
Sonography may be warranted to exclude cholelithiasis and biliary obstruction (p. 1042).

75
00:09:23,301 --> 00:09:32,289
Moreover, acute viral hepatitis is an unlikely diagnosis because of the usually low serum transaminase levels seen with ICP.

76
00:09:32,289 --> 00:09:42,339
Conversely, underlying chronic hepatitis C is associated with a significantly greater risk of developing ICP, which may be as much as 20fold higher .

77
00:09:42,339 --> 00:09:49,151
Management Pruritus may be troublesome and is thought to result from elevated serum bile salt concentrations.

78
00:09:49,151 --> 00:09:52,439
Antihistamines and topical emollients

79
00:09:52,588 --> 00:09:55,100
usually provide some relief.

80
00:09:55,050 --> 00:10:02,475
Cholestyramine is reported to be effective, but this compound also lowers absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.

81
00:10:02,475 --> 00:10:07,250
This may lead to vitamin K deficiency and fetal coagulopathy.

82
00:10:07,250 --> 00:10:11,988
Subsequent fetal intracranial hemorrhage and stillbirth have been reported .

83
00:10:11,988 --> 00:10:26,675
Currently, the most popular treatment is with ursodeoxycholic acid (Actigall), which relieves pruritus and reduces serum levels of bile salts and liver enzymes (Bacq, 2012; Parízek, 2016).

84
00:10:26,675 --> 00:10:30,475
It is available as 300-mg capsules.

85
00:10:30,475 --> 00:10:38,550
Oral dosing is 10 to 15 mg/kg maternal body weight daily, which is divided into two or three doses .

86
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:47,000
In a large randomized trial, the maternal “itch score” was not significantly lower with ursodeoxycholic acid compared with placebo .

87
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:56,313
From our experiences at Parkland Hospital, however, pruritus typically improves after 2 to 3 weeks of ursodeoxycholic acid therapy .

88
00:10:56,313 --> 00:11:03,525
Such treatment has also been reported to lower risks for stillbirth and fetal distress and is discussed subsequently.

89
00:11:03,525 --> 00:11:12,375
Other described treatments include therapeutic plasma exchange and rifampin (Liu, 2018; Ovadia, 2018).

90
00:11:12,375 --> 00:11:18,288
A randomized trial comparing ursodeoxycholic acid and rifampicin is underway .

91
00:11:18,288 --> 00:11:24,950
Perinatal Outcomes Early reports describe excessive adverse fetal outcomes in women with ICP.

92
00:11:24,950 --> 00:11:34,325
However, data from the past two decades are ambiguous concerning increased perinatal mortality rates and whether close fetal surveillance is preventive.

93
00:11:34,325 --> 00:11:46,125
Sheiner and coworkers (2006) described no differences in perinatal outcomes in 376 affected pregnancies compared with their overall obstetrical population.

94
00:11:46,125 --> 00:11:52,788
However, rates of labor induction and cesarean delivery in affected women were significantly higher.

95
00:11:52,788 --> 00:12:07,663
In a study of 5477 women with ICP from a database of 1.2 million births, neonates had a lower 5-minute Apgar score, but the stillbirth rate was not greater (Wikström Shemer, 2013).

96
00:12:07,663 --> 00:12:15,425
The latter rate was thought to reflect a higher labor induction rate, which at the time of the study was recommended to avoid stillbirth.

97
00:12:15,425 --> 00:12:28,625
In some studies, complications developed more frequently in women with higher total bile acid levels (Di Mascio, 2019; Herrera, 2018; Ovadia, 2019).

98
00:12:28,625 --> 00:12:34,300
These studies described stillbirths despite normal findings during antenatal testing.

99
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:43,950
One study of 693 Swedish women found a greater perinatal mortality rate in women with bile acids levels >40 μmol/L .

100
00:12:43,950 --> 00:12:58,388
In others, an increased stillbirth risk was noted in women whose serum bile acid levels were >100 μmol/L (Brouwers, 2015; Chappell, 2019, Kawakita, 2015).

101
00:12:58,388 --> 00:13:07,013
Chappell and associates (2019) also found that ursodeoxycholic acid treatment did not lower perinatal death rates.

102
00:13:07,013 --> 00:13:11,000
The use of antenatal fetal testing was not described.

103
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:25,650
In a metaanalysis of 5557 women with ICP, Ovadia and colleagues (2019) concluded that serum bile acid levels >100 μmol/L were associated with stillbirth.

104
00:13:25,650 --> 00:13:29,738
Other adverse perinatal outcomes also have been described.

105
00:13:29,738 --> 00:13:43,963
Brouwers and colleagues (2015) found higher rates of spontaneous preterm birth (19 percent) and meconium-stained amnionic fluid (48 percent), despite active management leading to earlier delivery.

106
00:13:43,963 --> 00:13:55,100
Novel associations of ICP with preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, and large for gestational age also are reported (Wikström Shemer, 2013).

107
00:13:55,100 --> 00:14:02,563
In summary, management is directed at early delivery to mitigate the relatively uncommon incidence of fetal death.

108
00:14:02,563 --> 00:14:09,738
Treatment with ursodeoxycholic acid does not always improve maternal symptoms or protect against stillbirth.

109
00:14:09,738 --> 00:14:15,975
It is problematic that antenatal testing does not reliably forecast impending fetal death.

110
00:14:15,975 --> 00:14:20,500
We do not routinely employ these for uncomplicated ICP.

111
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:28,113
The precise gestational age for labor induction is uncertain, but many authors recommend 37 weeks’ gestation .

112
00:14:28,113 --> 00:14:47,975
At Parkland Hospital, we routinely induce labor at 38 weeks if symptoms worsen or if serum bile acid levels exceed 40 μmol/L. Fetal Effects of Bile Acids As noted, evidence supports that maternal serum bile acid levels >100 μmol/L contribute to fetal death and meconium passage.

113
00:14:47,975 --> 00:14:54,275
Fetal death may be related to the cardiotoxicity of bile acids, which causes cardiac

114
00:14:54,436 --> 00:14:57,973
dysfunction and presumably cardiac arrest.

115
00:14:57,923 --> 00:15:08,773
In an ex-vivo preparation of cardiac myocytes, cholic acid lowered beating rates in a dose-dependent fashion, while elevating intracellular calcium concentration .

116
00:15:08,773 --> 00:15:20,686
Prolongation of the fetal cardiac PR interval and successful reversal with ursodeoxycholic acid has been described (Rodríguez, 2018; Strehlow, 2010).

117
00:15:20,686 --> 00:15:30,873
Last, Ozel and coworkers (2018) demonstrated impaired global ventricular function in fetuses of pregnancies complicated by ICP.

118
00:15:30,873 --> 00:15:34,223
# Acute Fatty Liver of Pregnancy.

119
00:15:34,223 --> 00:15:43,736
The most frequent cause of acute liver failure during pregnancy is acute fatty liver—also called acute fatty metamorphosis or acute yellow atrophy.

120
00:15:43,736 --> 00:15:51,286
It is characterized by accumulation of microvesicular fat that literally “crowds out” normal hepatocytic function .

121
00:15:51,286 --> 00:15:55,948
Grossly, the liver is small, soft, yellow, and greasy.

122
00:15:55,948 --> 00:16:01,473
In its worst form, the incidence approximates 1 case per 10,000 births .

123
00:16:01,473 --> 00:16:05,536
AFLP recurring in subsequent pregnancy is rare .

124
00:16:05,536 --> 00:16:08,073
Figure 58-1.

125
00:16:08,073 --> 00:16:10,936
Acute fatty liver of pregnancy.

126
00:16:10,936 --> 00:16:17,836
Cross section of the liver from a woman who died as the result of pulmonary aspiration and respiratory failure.

127
00:16:17,836 --> 00:16:20,923
The liver has a greasy yellow appearance.

128
00:16:20,923 --> 00:16:28,423
Inset: Electron photomicrograph of a swollen hepatocyte containing numerous microvesicular fat droplets ().

129
00:16:28,423 --> 00:16:35,711
The nucleus (N) remains centered within the cell in contrast to the case with macrovesicular fat deposition.

130
00:16:35,711 --> 00:16:39,298
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Don Wheeler.

131
00:16:39,298 --> 00:16:51,811
Etiopathogenesis Although the underlying cause remains unclear, many cases of AFLP are associated with recessively inherited mitochondrial abnormalities of fatty acid oxidation.

132
00:16:51,811 --> 00:17:01,236
Several mutations for the mitochondrial trifunctional protein enzyme complex that catalyzes the last oxidative steps in the pathway are implicated.

133
00:17:01,236 --> 00:17:14,286
The most common are the G1528C and E474Q missense mutations of the gene on chromosome 2 that codes for long-chain-3hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (LCHAD) .

134
00:17:14,286 --> 00:17:24,936
Mutations for medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) and for carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1 (CPT1) deficiency are others.

135
00:17:24,936 --> 00:17:29,373
These are similar to mutations in children with Reye-like syndromes.

136
00:17:29,373 --> 00:17:39,936
Sims and colleagues (1995) observed that some homozygous LCHADdeficient children with Reye-like syndromes had heterozygous mothers with AFLP.

137
00:17:39,936 --> 00:17:44,448
This was also seen in women with a compound heterozygous fetus.

138
00:17:44,448 --> 00:17:54,298
Although some conclude that only heterozygous LCHAD-deficient mothers are at risk for ALFP when their fetus is homozygous, this is not always true .

139
00:17:54,298 --> 00:18:05,511
An association between fatty acid β-oxidation enzyme defects and severe preeclampsia, especially in women with HELLP syndrome, is also controversial .

140
00:18:05,511 --> 00:18:12,511
Most observations derive from retrospective study of mothers with a child who later developed Reye-like syndrome.

141
00:18:12,511 --> 00:18:21,436
One case-control study compared 50 mothers of children with a fatty acid oxidation defect and 1250 mothers of matched control infants .

142
00:18:21,436 --> 00:18:30,411
During their pregnancy, 12 percent of mothers with an affected child developed HELLP syndrome and 4 percent developed AFLP.

143
00:18:30,411 --> 00:18:36,248
Comparatively, only 0.9 percent of control women developed liver problems.

144
00:18:36,248 --> 00:18:56,811
Despite these findings, the clinical, biochemical, and histopathological findings are sufficiently disparate and support that severe preeclampsia, with or without HELLP syndrome, and AFLP are distinct syndromes (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2012; Byrne, 2020).

145
00:18:56,811 --> 00:19:03,898
Clinical Findings AFLP almost always manifests in the last trimester and rarely midpregnancy .

146
00:19:03,898 --> 00:19:17,673
From Parkland Hospital, Nelson and colleagues (2013) described 51 women with AFLP at a mean gestational age of 37 weeks (range 31.7 to 40.9 weeks).

147
00:19:17,673 --> 00:19:25,323
Approximately 41 percent were nulliparous and 20 percent were delivered at 34 weeks’ gestation or earlier.

148
00:19:25,323 --> 00:19:30,148
Women with a multifetal gestation account for 20 percent of cases .

149
00:19:30,148 --> 00:19:37,773
AFLP has a broad spectrum of clinical features and severity, and symptoms usually advance over several days.

150
00:19:37,773 --> 00:19:41,561
Persistent nausea and vomiting are major complaints.

151
00:19:41,561 --> 00:19:47,536
Degrees of malaise, anorexia, epigastric pain, and progressive jaundice vary.

152
00:19:47,536 --> 00:19:53,798
In almost all severe cases, acute liver injury causes profound endothelial cell

153
00:19:53,956 --> 00:20:03,493
activation, capillary leakage, and hemoconcentration; acute kidney injury; ascites; and sometimes pulmonary permeability edema .

154
00:20:03,443 --> 00:20:10,193
Half of affected women have hypertension, proteinuria, and edema, alone or in combination.

155
00:20:10,193 --> 00:20:14,281
Notably, these signs also suggest preeclampsia.

156
00:20:14,281 --> 00:20:27,106
As shown in Tables 58-1 and 58-2, degrees of moderate to severe liver dysfunction are manifest by hypofibrinogenemia, hypocholesterolemia, and prolonged clotting times.

157
00:20:27,106 --> 00:20:38,231
Serum bilirubin levels usually are <10 mg/dL, and serum transaminase levels are modestly elevated and usually <1000 U/L.

158
00:20:38,231 --> 00:20:43,568
An AFLP diagnosis is arrived at by clinical and laboratory findings.

159
00:20:43,568 --> 00:20:46,193
Biopsy is unnecessary.

160
00:20:46,193 --> 00:20:59,656
The Swansea criteria proposed by Ch’ng and associates (2002) as a screening tool and predictor of severity shows suitable sensitivity (Morton, 2018; Wang, 2017).

161
00:20:59,656 --> 00:21:02,106
Table 58-2.

162
00:21:02,106 --> 00:21:13,456
Comparison of Clinical Findings at the Time of Admission in 67 Women with Acute Fatty Liver of Pregnancy and 67 with HELLP Syndrome Table 58-3.

163
00:21:13,456 --> 00:21:23,531
Swansea Criteria for AFLP Diagnosisa Hemolysis can be severe and is thought to stem from effects of hypocholesterolemia on erythrocyte membranes .

164
00:21:23,531 --> 00:21:37,468
Laboratory evidence includes leukocytosis, nucleated red cells, mild to moderate thrombocytopenia, and increased serum levels of lactic acid dehydrogenase (LDH) or decreased haptoglobin levels.

165
00:21:37,468 --> 00:21:41,693
The peripheral blood smear demonstrates echinocytosis.

166
00:21:41,693 --> 00:21:48,106
However, the hematocrit is often high or within the normal range because of hemoconcentration.

167
00:21:48,106 --> 00:21:57,068
With severe hemoconcentration, uteroplacental perfusion is reduced and this, along with maternal acidosis, can cause fetal death.

168
00:21:57,068 --> 00:22:06,368
Likewise, maternal and fetal acidemia are associated with a high incidence of fetal jeopardy and a concordantly high cesarean delivery rate.

169
00:22:06,368 --> 00:22:14,318
The degree of clotting dysfunction varies and can be potentially life threatening, especially if cesarean delivery is undertaken.

170
00:22:14,318 --> 00:22:25,256
Coagulopathy is caused by diminished hepatic procoagulant synthesis, although some evidence supports increased consumption from disseminated intravascular coagulopathy.

171
00:22:25,256 --> 00:22:30,881
As shown in Table 58-2, hypofibrinogenemia can be profound.

172
00:22:30,881 --> 00:22:40,456
Of 51 women cared for at Parkland Hospital, almost one third had a plasma fibrinogen level nadir below 100 mg/dL .

173
00:22:40,456 --> 00:22:48,393
Elevated levels of serum D-dimers or fibrin-split products also indicate an element of consumptive coagulopathy .

174
00:22:48,393 --> 00:22:53,193
Although usually modest, occasionally thrombocytopenia is marked.

175
00:22:53,193 --> 00:23:05,243
Among the women from Parkland Hospital, 20 percent had platelet counts <100,000/μL, and 10 percent had platelet counts <50,000/μL .

176
00:23:05,243 --> 00:23:37,856
Because liver dysfunction and kidney injury are central to AFLP, Byrne and colleagues (2020) have proposed the acronym FaCCT to differentiate AFLP from HELLP syndrome using levels of commonly available laboratory analytes: fibrinogen (<300 mg/dL) and cholesterol (<220 mg/dL), creatinine (>0.9 mg/dL), and total bilirubin (>1 mg/dL).

177
00:23:37,856 --> 00:23:51,243
To emphasize, the clinical features and sequelae of AFLP are attributed to liver dysfunction, whereas in HELLP syndrome the procoagulant production and function of the liver appears relatively preserved.

178
00:23:51,243 --> 00:23:55,818
AFLP typically continues to worsen after diagnosis.

179
00:23:55,818 --> 00:24:06,368
Hypoglycemia is common, and hepatic encephalopathy, severe coagulopathy, and some degree of renal failure each develop in approximately half of women.

180
00:24:06,368 --> 00:24:13,968
Delivery fortunately arrests liver function deterioration, but recovery may require substantial supportive care.

181
00:24:13,968 --> 00:24:20,806
Various liver imaging techniques can help confirm the diagnosis, but none is particularly reliable .

182
00:24:20,806 --> 00:24:33,031
In a prospective evaluation of the Swansea criteria, only a quarter of women with AFLP had classic sonographic findings that included maternal ascites or an echogenic hepatic appearance .

183
00:24:33,031 --> 00:24:42,106
Although Châtel and coworkers (2016) described greater liver fat detected by MR imaging, this has not been our experience.

184
00:24:42,106 --> 00:24:46,993
We have encountered several women with an underdeveloped form of AFLP.

185
00:24:46,993 --> 00:24:57,143
Clinical involvement is relatively minor and laboratory aberrations— usually only hemolysis and a decreased plasma fibrinogen level—herald the syndrome.

186
00:24:57,143 --> 00:25:00,943
Thus, the spectrum of liver involvement can include

187
00:25:01,108 --> 00:25:11,858
unnoticed milder cases, findings that are instead attributed to preeclampsia, or a severe form displaying overt hepatic failure and associated encephalopathy.

188
00:25:11,808 --> 00:25:17,208
Management Intensive supportive measures and sound obstetrical care are essential.

189
00:25:17,208 --> 00:25:26,008
Delivery of the fetus is necessary in the treatment of AFLP, and significant procrastination increases maternal and fetal risks.

190
00:25:26,008 --> 00:25:30,533
We prefer a trial of labor induction with close fetal surveillance.

191
00:25:30,533 --> 00:25:39,920
Although some recommend cesarean delivery to hasten hepatic healing, this raises maternal bleeding complication risks when coagulopathy is severe.

192
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:47,283
Nonetheless, cesarean delivery is common due to fetal intolerance of labor, and rates approach 90 percent.

193
00:25:47,283 --> 00:25:55,183
In some cases, the fetus has already died when AFLP is diagnosed, and the delivery route is less problematic.

194
00:25:55,183 --> 00:26:09,120
To correct coagulopathy, transfusions with whole blood or packed red cells, along with fresh-frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate, and platelets, are usually necessary for surgery or vaginal laceration repair .

195
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:17,583
Hepatic function usually returns to normal within a week postpartum, but in the interim, intensive medical support may be required.

196
00:26:17,583 --> 00:26:21,483
Two associated conditions can be seen during this time.

197
00:26:21,483 --> 00:26:26,558
Perhaps a fourth of women have evidence for transient diabetes insipidus.

198
00:26:26,558 --> 00:26:35,183
This presumably stems from elevated vasopressinase concentrations caused by diminished hepatic production of its inactivating enzyme.

199
00:26:35,183 --> 00:26:40,320
Second, acute pancreatitis develops in approximately 20 percent.

200
00:26:40,320 --> 00:26:44,258
With supportive care, recovery usually is complete.

201
00:26:44,258 --> 00:26:54,120
Maternal deaths are caused by liver failure, sepsis, hemorrhage, aspiration, renal failure, pancreatitis, and gastrointestinal bleeding.

202
00:26:54,120 --> 00:26:57,758
Two women died in the series from Parkland Hospital.

203
00:26:57,758 --> 00:27:04,145
One had associated encephalopathy and aspirated before intubation during transfer to our care.

204
00:27:04,145 --> 00:27:09,320
The other was a woman with profound liver failure and nonresponsive hypotension .

205
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:18,295
Other treatment measures have included plasma exchange and liver transplantation (Ringers, 2016; Wu, 2018).

206
00:27:18,295 --> 00:27:28,420
Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes Maternal mortality rates with AFLP approached 75 percent in the past, but the contemporaneous outlook is much better.

207
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:35,145
From his review, Sibai (2007) cites an average mortality rate of 7 percent.

208
00:27:35,145 --> 00:27:44,345
He also cited a 70-percent preterm delivery rate and a 15-percent perinatal mortality rate, which in the past was nearly 90 percent.

209
00:27:44,345 --> 00:27:53,583
During the past four decades at Parkland Hospital, the maternal and perinatal mortality rates have been 4 percent and 12 percent, respectively .

210
00:27:53,583 --> 00:27:56,333
# Viral Hepatitis.

211
00:27:56,333 --> 00:28:04,883
Most viral hepatitis syndromes are asymptomatic, and acute symptomatic infections are becoming less common in the United States.

212
00:28:04,883 --> 00:28:20,583
There are at least five distinct types of viruses causing hepatitis: A (HAV), B (HBV), D (HDV) caused by the hepatitis B–associated delta agent, C (HCV), and E (HEV).

213
00:28:20,583 --> 00:28:35,420
The clinical presentation of acute infection is similar in all, and although the viruses themselves probably are not hepatotoxic, the immunological response to them causes hepatocellular necrosis (Dienstag, 2018a,b).

214
00:28:35,420 --> 00:28:46,883
Several other viral agents can infect the liver, and two examples are cytomegalovirus and herpes simplex virus (Calix, 2020; McCormack, 2019).

215
00:28:46,883 --> 00:28:51,470
Acute infections are most often subclinical and anicteric.

216
00:28:51,470 --> 00:28:58,995
When they are clinically apparent, nausea and vomiting, headache, and malaise may precede jaundice by 1 to 2 weeks.

217
00:28:58,995 --> 00:29:03,358
By the time jaundice develops, symptoms usually are improving.

218
00:29:03,358 --> 00:29:12,508
Low-grade fever is more common with hepatitis A. Serum transaminase levels vary, and their peaks do not correspond with disease severity.

219
00:29:12,508 --> 00:29:19,620
Peak levels that range from 400 to 4000 U/L are usually reached by the time jaundice develops .

220
00:29:19,620 --> 00:29:29,533
Serum bilirubin values typically continue to rise, and peak at 5 to 20 mg/dL, despite falling serum transaminase levels.

221
00:29:29,533 --> 00:29:32,958
Severe features should prompt hospitalization.

222
00:29:32,958 --> 00:29:44,858
These include persistent nausea and vomiting, prolonged prothrombin time, low serum albumin level, hypoglycemia, high serum bilirubin level, or central nervous system symptoms.

223
00:29:44,858 --> 00:29:51,933
In most cases, clinical and biochemical recovery is complete within 1 to 2 months in all cases of

224
00:29:52,084 --> 00:30:08,321
hepatitis A, in most cases of hepatitis B, but in only a small proportion of those caused by hepatitis C. Patient’s feces, secretions, bedpans, and other articles in contact with the intestinal tract should be handled with glove-protected hands.

225
00:30:08,321 --> 00:30:15,834
In hospitals, extra precautions, such as double gloving during delivery and surgical procedures, are recommended.

226
00:30:15,834 --> 00:30:29,896
Due to significant exposure of health-care personnel to hepatitis B, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommend active and passive vaccination, as described later (p. 1037) .

227
00:30:29,896 --> 00:30:36,434
For hepatitis C, although direct antiviral regimens have been developed, there is no vaccine.

228
00:30:36,434 --> 00:30:41,171
Guidelines instead recommend postexposure serosurveillance only.

229
00:30:41,171 --> 00:30:46,034
Acute hepatitis has a case-fatality rate of 0.1 percent.

230
00:30:46,034 --> 00:30:50,709
For those requiring hospitalization, it may reach 1 percent.

231
00:30:50,709 --> 00:30:57,959
Most fatalities are due to fulminant hepatic necrosis, which in later pregnancy may resemble AFLP.

232
00:30:57,959 --> 00:31:05,309
In these cases, hepatic encephalopathy is the usual presentation, and the mortality rate is 80 percent.

233
00:31:05,309 --> 00:31:13,571
Approximately half of patients with fulminant disease have hepatitis B infection, and co-infection with the delta agent is common.

234
00:31:13,571 --> 00:31:17,634
Chronic hepatitis is more prevalent than acute infection.

235
00:31:17,634 --> 00:31:27,246
The CDC (2019) estimates that almost 3.5 million persons in the United States are living with chronic viral hepatitis.

236
00:31:27,246 --> 00:31:34,246
Although most are asymptomatic, approximately 20 percent develop cirrhosis within 10 to 20 years .

237
00:31:34,246 --> 00:31:39,071
When present, symptoms are nonspecific and usually include fatigue.

238
00:31:39,071 --> 00:31:45,421
In some patients, cirrhosis with liver failure or bleeding varices may be the initial finding.

239
00:31:45,421 --> 00:31:54,484
Also, asymptomatic chronic viral hepatitis remains the leading cause of liver cancer and the most frequent reason for liver transplantation.

240
00:31:54,484 --> 00:31:59,059
Chronic viral hepatitis is usually diagnosed serologically .

241
00:31:59,059 --> 00:32:10,271
With persistently abnormal biochemical tests, liver biopsy usually discloses active inflammation, continuing necrosis, and fibrosis that may lead to cirrhosis.

242
00:32:10,271 --> 00:32:19,096
Chronic hepatitis is classified by cause; grade, defined by histological activity; and stage, which is the degree of progression .

243
00:32:19,096 --> 00:32:21,659
Table 58-4.

244
00:32:21,659 --> 00:32:30,609
Laboratory Features of Chronic Hepatitis Most young women with chronic viral hepatitis either are asymptomatic or have only mild liver disease.

245
00:32:30,609 --> 00:32:35,496
For asymptomatic women, pregnancies are usually uncomplicated.

246
00:32:35,496 --> 00:32:46,671
With symptomatic chronic active hepatitis, pregnancy outcome depends primarily on disease severity, and especially on the presence of portal hypertension (p. 1040).

247
00:32:46,671 --> 00:32:52,321
The few women whom we have managed have done well, but their long-term prognosis is poor.

248
00:32:52,321 --> 00:33:00,384
Accordingly, they should be counseled regarding possible liver transplantation as well as contraceptive and sterilization options.

249
00:33:00,384 --> 00:33:06,684
Hepatitis A Vaccination has reduced the incidence of hepatitis A by 95 percent.

250
00:33:06,684 --> 00:33:13,621
In 2019, the rate was 6 cases per 100,000 individuals in the United States .

251
00:33:13,621 --> 00:33:22,296
HAV is an RNA picornavirus and is transmitted by the fecal–oral route and usually by contaminated food or water ingestion.

252
00:33:22,296 --> 00:33:26,046
The incubation period approximates 4 weeks.

253
00:33:26,046 --> 00:33:34,284
Individuals shed virus in their feces, and during the relatively brief period of viremia, their blood also is infectious.

254
00:33:34,284 --> 00:33:41,321
Signs and symptoms are often nonspecific and usually mild, although jaundice develops in most patients.

255
00:33:41,321 --> 00:33:50,234
Symptoms usually last less than 2 months, but 10 to 15 percent of patients may remain symptomatic or relapse for up to 6 months .

256
00:33:50,234 --> 00:33:59,134
Early serological testing identifies immunoglobulin M (IgM) anti-HAV antibody, which may persist for several months.

257
00:33:59,134 --> 00:34:05,596
During convalescence, IgG antibody predominates, and it provides subsequent immunity.

258
00:34:05,596 --> 00:34:08,696
Hepatitis A lacks a chronic stage.

259
00:34:08,696 --> 00:34:15,734
Vaccination during childhood with inactivated hepatitis viral vaccine is more than 90-percent effective.

260
00:34:15,734 --> 00:34:27,409
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) and the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices recommends HAV vaccination for high-risk adults .

261
00:34:27,409 --> 00:34:30,384
This includes pregnant women at risk .

262
00:34:30,384 --> 00:34:39,884
Candidates are persons with chronic liver disease, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, homelessness, or illegal drug use.

263
00:34:39,884 --> 00:34:42,046
Those working in group

264
00:34:42,196 --> 00:34:51,258
facilities with at-risk persons, conducting research with HAV, or traveling to or adopting from endemic areas also are included.

265
00:34:51,208 --> 00:34:59,221
High-risk countries are listed in the CDC (2020b) “Yellow Book”—Health Information for International Travel.

266
00:34:59,221 --> 00:35:13,446
For an unvaccinated gravida who is recently exposed by close personal or sexual contact with a person with hepatitis A, passive immunization is provided by a 0.1-mL/kg dose of immune globulin.

267
00:35:13,446 --> 00:35:19,583
Concurrently, the first dose of the hepatitis A vaccine series is given in a separate arm .

268
00:35:19,583 --> 00:35:27,933
Pregnancy and Hepatitis A. Management of hepatitis A in pregnancy includes a balanced diet and diminished physical activity.

269
00:35:27,933 --> 00:35:32,008
Women with mild illness may be managed as outpatients.

270
00:35:32,008 --> 00:35:43,871
In developed countries, the effects of hepatitis A on pregnancy outcomes are not dramatic (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2012, 2018).

271
00:35:43,871 --> 00:35:51,108
However, in resource-poor countries, both perinatal and maternal mortality rates are substantively higher.

272
00:35:51,108 --> 00:35:56,421
HAV is not teratogenic, and transmission to the fetus is negligible.

273
00:35:56,421 --> 00:36:02,271
The risk of preterm birth may be higher, and neonatal cholestasis has been reported .

274
00:36:02,271 --> 00:36:11,383
Although HAV RNA has been isolated in breast milk, no cases of neonatal hepatitis A have been reported secondary to breastfeeding .

275
00:36:11,383 --> 00:36:17,558
Hepatitis B This infection stems from a double-stranded DNA virus that is found worldwide.

276
00:36:17,558 --> 00:36:27,183
HBV is endemic in Africa, Central and Southeast Asia, China, Eastern Europe, the Middle East, and certain areas of South America.

277
00:36:27,183 --> 00:36:31,808
In all these, infection prevalence reaches 5 to 20 percent.

278
00:36:31,808 --> 00:36:37,871
An estimated 1.6 million people in the United States have chronic hepatitis B .

279
00:36:37,871 --> 00:36:44,608
HBV is carcinogenic, and chronic infection is a known risk for hepatocellular carcinoma.

280
00:36:44,608 --> 00:36:48,583
For others, cirrhosis can be a long-term sequela.

281
00:36:48,583 --> 00:36:54,933
HBV is transmitted by exposure to blood or other body fluids from infected individuals.

282
00:36:54,933 --> 00:37:05,396
In endemic countries, vertical transmission, that is from mother to fetus or newborn, accounts for at least 35 to 50 percent of chronic HBV infections.

283
00:37:05,396 --> 00:37:16,346
In low-prevalence countries such as the United States, which has a prevalence below 2 percent, the more frequent transmission mode is sexual contact or shared contaminated needles.

284
00:37:16,346 --> 00:37:23,221
Acute hepatitis B develops after an incubation period that ranges from 40 to 150 days .

285
00:37:23,221 --> 00:37:27,071
At least half of acute infections are asymptomatic.

286
00:37:27,071 --> 00:37:31,333
Symptoms completely resolve within 3 to 4 months in most.

287
00:37:31,333 --> 00:37:36,958
However, acute hepatitis B accounts for half of fulminant hepatitis cases.

288
00:37:36,958 --> 00:37:39,446
Figure 58-2.

289
00:37:39,446 --> 00:37:45,571
details the sequence of the various HBV antigens and antibodies in acute infection.

290
00:37:45,571 --> 00:37:54,621
The first serological marker is hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), which often precedes the rise in transaminase levels.

291
00:37:54,621 --> 00:38:03,646
As HBsAg disappears, antibodies to the surface antigen develop (anti-HBs), and this marks complete disease resolution.

292
00:38:03,646 --> 00:38:11,183
Hepatitis B core antigen (HBcAg) is an intracellular antigen and not detectable in serum.

293
00:38:11,183 --> 00:38:20,121
However, antibodies against this core antigen (anti-HBc) are detectable and found within weeks of HBsAg appearance.

294
00:38:20,121 --> 00:38:30,171
The hepatitis B e antigen (HBeAg) is present during times of high viral replication and often correlates with detectable HBV DNA.

295
00:38:30,171 --> 00:38:40,721
After acute hepatitis, at least 90 percent of adults recover completely, and the remainder are considered to have chronic hepatitis B. Figure 58-2.

296
00:38:40,721 --> 00:39:07,046
Sequence of various antigens and antibodies in acute hepatitis B. ALT = alanine transaminase; anti-HBc = antibody to hepatitis B core antigen; anti-HBe = antibody to hepatitis B e antigen; anti-HBs = antibody to hepatitis B surface antigen; HBeAg = hepatitis B e antigen; HBsAg = hepatitis B surface antigen.

297
00:39:07,046 --> 00:39:11,796
Redrawn from Dienstag JL: Acute viral hepatitis.

298
00:39:11,796 --> 00:39:20,958
In Jameson JL, Fauci AS, Kasper DL, et al (eds): Harrison’s Principles of Internal Medicine, 20th ed.

299
00:39:20,958 --> 00:39:27,058
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2018b, p 2347.

300
00:39:27,058 --> 00:39:36,108
Chronic HBV infection is often asymptomatic but may cause persistent anorexia, weight loss, fatigue, and hepatosplenomegaly.

301
00:39:36,108 --> 00:39:48,771
Extrahepatic manifestations include arthritis, generalized vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, pericarditis, myocarditis, transverse myelitis, and peripheral neuropathy.

302
00:39:48,771 --> 00:39:51,133
One risk factor for

303
00:39:51,292 --> 00:39:54,379
chronic disease is age at acquisition.

304
00:39:54,329 --> 00:39:59,654
Pertinent to the obstetrician, this risk exceeds 90 percent in newborns.

305
00:39:59,654 --> 00:40:06,029
The risk is 50 percent in young children and is less than 10 percent in immunocompetent adults .

306
00:40:06,029 --> 00:40:14,529
An immunocompromised state such as with HIV infection, organ transplantation, or chemotherapy creates vulnerability.

307
00:40:14,529 --> 00:40:21,179
Those with chronic HBV infection have serum test results that show HBsAg persistence.

308
00:40:21,179 --> 00:40:30,079
These infected persons may be asymptomatic carriers or may have chronic liver disease with or without cirrhosis or hepatocellular cancer.

309
00:40:30,079 --> 00:40:35,979
The presence of HPV DNA in serum tests is a marker of HBV replication.

310
00:40:35,979 --> 00:40:48,804
Those with high HBV replication, which is reflected in high HBV DNA levels with or without HBeAg, have the greatest likelihood of developing cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma.

311
00:40:48,804 --> 00:40:51,442
Pregnancy and Hepatitis B.

312
00:40:51,442 --> 00:40:59,654
The US Preventive Services Task Force (2019) recommends that all pregnant women be screened for HBV.

313
00:40:59,654 --> 00:41:05,779
This practice identifies asymptomatic cases and later allows neonatal intervention.

314
00:41:05,779 --> 00:41:15,204
At the first prenatal care visit, a serologic HBsAg level is obtained, and this is repeated in high-risk women again at the time of delivery.

315
00:41:15,204 --> 00:41:22,954
All states require cases of HBV infection to be reported, and 26 mandate that pregnant women be screened .

316
00:41:22,954 --> 00:41:28,754
HBV infection does not cause excessive rates of maternal morbidity or mortality .

317
00:41:28,754 --> 00:41:40,917
In one regional study in the United States, 14 percent of women with HBV infection had a worsening of disease, sometimes termed a flare, either antepartum or in the 6 months postpartum .

318
00:41:40,917 --> 00:41:52,904
A review of data from the National Inpatient Sample reported a modest rise in preterm birth rates in HBV-positive mothers but no effect on fetal-growth restriction or preeclampsia rates .

319
00:41:52,904 --> 00:41:59,542
Others have shown similar results (Cui, 2017; Liu, 2017b).

320
00:41:59,542 --> 00:42:11,017
Transplacental HBV infection is uncommon, and Towers and associates (2001) reported that viral DNA is rarely found in amnionic fluid or cord blood.

321
00:42:11,017 --> 00:42:23,054
The highest HBV DNA levels are found in women who transmitted the virus to their fetuses (Dunkelberg, 2014; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2016).

322
00:42:23,054 --> 00:42:36,892
To curb vertical transmission, newborns of seropositive mothers are given hepatitis B immune globulin (HBIG) plus the first of a three-dose hepatitis B recombinant vaccine series very soon after birth.

323
00:42:36,892 --> 00:42:46,729
In the absence of HBV immunoprophylaxis, 10 to 20 percent of women positive for HBsAg transmit viral infection to their infant.

324
00:42:46,729 --> 00:42:53,404
This rate rises to almost 90 percent if the mother is HBsAg and HBeAg positive.

325
00:42:53,404 --> 00:43:04,392
Administration of immunoprophylaxis and hepatitis B vaccine shortly after birth has lowered transmission dramatically and prevented approximately 90 percent of infections .

326
00:43:04,392 --> 00:43:22,592
But, women with high HBV viral loads—106 to 108 copies/mL—or those who are HBeAg positive still have at least a 10percent vertical transmission rate, regardless of immunoprophylaxis (Rogan, 2019; Yi, 2016).

327
00:43:22,592 --> 00:43:32,467
Hill and colleagues (2002) reported that the 2.4-percent transmission rate was not increased with breastfeeding if neonatal vaccination was completed.

328
00:43:32,467 --> 00:43:39,342
Although virus is present in breast milk, the incidence of transmission is not diminished by formula feeding .

329
00:43:39,342 --> 00:43:51,742
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) do not consider maternal HBV infection a contraindication to breastfeeding.

330
00:43:51,742 --> 00:44:06,292
As a second transmission-prevention practice, the CDC and numerous societies now recommend antiviral therapy to decrease viral levels in gravidas whose viral load exceeds 106 to 108 copies/mL .

331
00:44:06,292 --> 00:44:13,842
Newer drugs include tenofovir, an adenosine nucleoside analogue, and telbivudine, a thymidine analogue.

332
00:44:13,842 --> 00:44:20,017
Tenofovir is the first-line agent during pregnancy, and no dose adjustments are necessary .

333
00:44:20,017 --> 00:44:29,879
These antiviral medications appear safe in pregnancy and are not associated with greater rates of congenital malformations or adverse obstetrical outcomes .

334
00:44:29,879 --> 00:44:35,079
Follow-up of children at age 6 to 7 years found no related problems .

335
00:44:35,079 --> 00:44:45,267
Regarding efficacy, one randomized trial in Thailand showed that tenofovir compared with placebo beginning at 28 weeks’ gestation did not reduce the low rate of

336
00:44:45,436 --> 00:44:52,023
vertical transmission if both active and passive immunoprophylaxis were given at birth to the neonate .

337
00:44:51,973 --> 00:44:58,098
In another similar randomized trial, vertical transmission rates were significantly lowered .

338
00:44:58,098 --> 00:45:04,236
For high-risk mothers who are seronegative, HBV vaccine can be given during pregnancy.

339
00:45:04,236 --> 00:45:18,261
Candidates are persons with chronic liver disease, diabetes, HIV or HCV infection, injection drug use, occupational exposure risk, household contacts, or long-term travel to endemic areas.

340
00:45:18,261 --> 00:45:28,986
Those requiring dialysis, living in a long-term-care or correctional facility, or engaging in sex with multiple or HBV-infected partners also are included .

341
00:45:28,986 --> 00:45:38,161
Vaccine efficacy is similar to that for nonpregnant adults, and overall seroconversion rates approach 95 percent after three doses .

342
00:45:38,161 --> 00:45:48,086
The traditional vaccination schedule of 0, 1, and 6 months may be difficult to complete during pregnancy, and compliance rates decline postpartum.

343
00:45:48,086 --> 00:46:02,848
Sheffield and coworkers (2006) reported that an accelerated three-dose regimen—given initially and at 1 and 4 months—resulted in seroconversion rates of 56, 77, and 90 percent, respectively.

344
00:46:02,848 --> 00:46:07,323
This regimen was usually easily completed during prenatal care.

345
00:46:07,323 --> 00:46:17,148
Hepatitis D Also called delta hepatitis, this is a defective RNA virus that is a hybrid particle with an HBsAg coat and a delta core.

346
00:46:17,148 --> 00:46:25,736
The HDV must co-infect with HBV either simultaneously or secondarily, and transmission is similar to HBV.

347
00:46:25,736 --> 00:46:30,773
Hepatitis C This infection stems from a single-stranded RNA virus.

348
00:46:30,773 --> 00:46:37,198
Transmission is via blood and other body fluids, although sexual transmission is inefficient.

349
00:46:37,198 --> 00:46:47,761
In the United States, adult prevalence of HCV was reported to be 2 to 5 cases per 1000 births (Prasad, 2020; Rossi, 2020).

350
00:46:47,761 --> 00:46:53,686
Up to a third of anti-HCV positive persons have no identifiable risk factors .

351
00:46:53,686 --> 00:46:59,898
Until recently, screening for HCV was encouraged only for high-risk individuals.

352
00:46:59,898 --> 00:47:09,736
But in 2020, the CDC recommended HCV screening for all adults at least once in a lifetime, and more regularly for those with risk factors .

353
00:47:09,736 --> 00:47:16,086
These are injection drug use, hemodialysis, or exposure to blood-contaminated items.

354
00:47:16,086 --> 00:47:22,786
The CDC also recommended prenatal HCV screening for all women regardless of risk factors.

355
00:47:22,786 --> 00:47:29,386
The US Preventive Services Task Force (2020) has published similar recommendations.

356
00:47:29,386 --> 00:47:37,336
Chronic hepatitis C is diagnosed by identification of anti-HCV antibodies detected with a serum immunoassay.

357
00:47:37,336 --> 00:47:43,073
Acute HCV infection is usually asymptomatic or yields mild symptoms.

358
00:47:43,073 --> 00:47:47,261
Only 10 to 15 percent of patients develop jaundice.

359
00:47:47,261 --> 00:47:53,848
The incubation period ranges from 15 to 160 days, with a mean of 7 weeks.

360
00:47:53,848 --> 00:47:59,023
Transaminase levels are elevated episodically during the acute infection.

361
00:47:59,023 --> 00:48:05,636
HCV RNA testing confirms clinical suspicion of active or acute HCV infection.

362
00:48:05,636 --> 00:48:11,948
RNA levels may be found even before rises in transaminase and anti-HCV levels.

363
00:48:11,948 --> 00:48:19,073
Anti-HCV antibody is not detected for an average of 15 weeks and in some cases up to 1 year .

364
00:48:19,073 --> 00:48:24,786
Nearly 80 to 90 percent of patients with acute HCV will be chronically infected.

365
00:48:24,786 --> 00:48:32,773
Although most remain asymptomatic, approximately 20 to 30 percent progress to cirrhosis within 20 to 30 years.

366
00:48:32,773 --> 00:48:37,761
Transaminase and HCV RNA values fluctuate over time.

367
00:48:37,761 --> 00:48:45,886
Liver biopsy reveals chronic disease and fibrosis in up to 50 percent, however, these findings are often mild.

368
00:48:45,886 --> 00:48:50,411
Overall, the long-term prognosis for most patients is good.

369
00:48:50,411 --> 00:48:53,073
Pregnancy and Hepatitis C.

370
00:48:53,073 --> 00:48:58,861
As expected, most pregnant women diagnosed with HCV have chronic disease.

371
00:48:58,861 --> 00:49:15,023
Aggregate reports have chronicled modestly greater fetal risks of low birthweight, neonatal intensive-care admission, preterm delivery, and mechanical ventilation (Rossi, 2020; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2017).

372
00:49:15,023 --> 00:49:23,223
In some women, these adverse outcomes may have been influenced by concurrent high-risk behaviors associated with HCV infection.

373
00:49:23,223 --> 00:49:30,123
The primary adverse perinatal outcome is vertical transmission of HCV infection to the fetus-neonate.

374
00:49:30,123 --> 00:49:33,098
This is higher in mothers with viremia .

375
00:49:33,098 --> 00:49:44,611
Airoldi and Berghella (2006) cited a rate of 1 to 3 percent in HCVpositive, RNA-negative women compared with 4 to 6 percent in those who were RNA-positive.

376
00:49:44,611 --> 00:49:47,611
In a report from Dublin, the vertical

377
00:49:47,764 --> 00:49:57,914
transmission rate in 545 women with HCV infection was 7 percent in RNA-positive women compared with none in those who were RNA-negative .

378
00:49:57,864 --> 00:50:07,401
Some have found an even higher risk when the mother is co-infected with HIV (Snidjewind, 2015; Tovo, 2016).

379
00:50:07,401 --> 00:50:12,564
Approximately two thirds of prenatal transmission cases occur peripartum.

380
00:50:12,564 --> 00:50:21,251
HCV genotype, invasive prenatal procedures, breastfeeding, and delivery mode are not associated with vertical transmission.

381
00:50:21,251 --> 00:50:27,589
That said, invasive procedures such as scalp-electrode fetal heart rate monitoring are avoided.

382
00:50:27,589 --> 00:50:31,889
HCV infection is not a contraindication to breastfeeding .

383
00:50:31,889 --> 00:50:36,226
No licensed vaccine is available for HCV prevention.

384
00:50:36,226 --> 00:50:42,851
In 2011, the first direct-acting antiviral drugs against HCV became available.

385
00:50:42,851 --> 00:50:51,976
Since then, second-generation prototypes with fewer adverse effects have been developed and are given as combinations to nonpregnant individuals.

386
00:50:51,976 --> 00:50:57,364
They are currently not recommended for use in pregnancy except in clinical trials .

387
00:50:57,364 --> 00:51:05,289
The combination of ledipasvir plus sofosbuvir shows efficacy and reassuring safety data in early clinical trials.

388
00:51:05,289 --> 00:51:14,489
Hepatitis E This infection stems from a water-borne RNA virus, which usually is enterically transmitted by contaminated water supplies.

389
00:51:14,489 --> 00:51:24,651
Hepatitis E is found worldwide, but the Centers for Disease Control (2020a) lists Mexico, Asia, and Africa as endemic areas.

390
00:51:24,651 --> 00:51:32,501
In these regions, seroprevalence rates vary by age and geography, but overall rates of 10 percent have been reported.

391
00:51:32,501 --> 00:51:37,664
In Mexico, Durango State has the highest rate—37 percent .

392
00:51:37,664 --> 00:51:45,476
Hepatitis E is the most common cause of acute hepatitis worldwide but rarely causes liver failure in the United States .

393
00:51:45,476 --> 00:51:51,889
Epidemic outbreaks in third-world countries result in substantial morbidity and mortality rates.

394
00:51:51,889 --> 00:51:57,076
Pregnant women have a greater case-fatality rate than nonpregnant individuals .

395
00:51:57,076 --> 00:52:07,239
In a metaanalysis of nearly 4000 women from Asia and Africa, maternal and fetal case-fatality rates were 21 and 34 percent, respectively .

396
00:52:07,239 --> 00:52:14,651
Fulminant hepatitis, although rare, is more common in gravidas and contributes to the increased mortality rates.

397
00:52:14,651 --> 00:52:19,664
A recombinant HEV vaccine has been developed and licensed in China .

398
00:52:19,664 --> 00:52:24,814
It is >95 percent effective for 12 months after vaccination.

399
00:52:24,814 --> 00:52:32,289
Long-term efficacy is 87 percent, and protective titers are maintained for up to 4.5 years .

400
00:52:32,289 --> 00:52:39,314
Preliminary data from inadvertently vaccinated pregnant women show no adverse maternal or fetal events .

401
00:52:39,314 --> 00:52:42,239
# Autoimmune Hepatitis.

402
00:52:42,239 --> 00:52:48,101
This generally progressive chronic hepatitis is more common in women and western countries.

403
00:52:48,101 --> 00:52:57,426
Autoimmune hepatitis frequently coexists with other types of autoimmune disease, particularly autoimmune thyroid disease and Sjögren syndrome.

404
00:52:57,426 --> 00:53:09,501
It is characterized by multiple autoimmune antibodies such as antinuclear (ANA), anti-smooth muscle, and anti-liver, kidney microsome 1 (LKM1) antibodies .

405
00:53:09,501 --> 00:53:13,151
Rates of subsequent cirrhosis vary worldwide.

406
00:53:13,151 --> 00:53:21,364
The less common type 2 autoimmune hepatitis has an even higher prevalence in females and typically a more aggressive presentation.

407
00:53:21,364 --> 00:53:25,014
The incidence peaks in childhood and adolescence.

408
00:53:25,014 --> 00:53:31,614
Symptoms are typical of acute and chronic hepatitis, but a fourth of patients may be asymptomatic.

409
00:53:31,614 --> 00:53:36,876
Treatment employs corticosteroids, alone or combined with azathioprine .

410
00:53:36,876 --> 00:53:42,376
Failure to respond to these two agents is more frequent in those with type 2 disease.

411
00:53:42,376 --> 00:53:47,489
Nearly all women with type 2 disease require long-term intensive therapy.

412
00:53:47,489 --> 00:53:53,914
In some patients with progressive disease and cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma develops.

413
00:53:53,914 --> 00:54:01,476
In general, autoimmune hepatitis—especially when severe—increases the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes.

414
00:54:01,476 --> 00:54:08,914
Westbrook and coworkers (2012) reported the outcomes of 81 pregnancies in 53 women.

415
00:54:08,914 --> 00:54:16,951
A third had a flare, and these were more common in those not taking medication and those with active disease in the year before conception.

416
00:54:16,951 --> 00:54:21,389
Women with cirrhosis had more maternal and fetal complications.

417
00:54:21,389 --> 00:54:34,026
From a Swedish national database of 171 births, the frequencies of preterm birth, low birthweight, and diabetes were elevated, but not those of preeclampsia or cesarean delivery .

418
00:54:34,026 --> 00:54:38,126
Another study reported delivery before 38 weeks’

419
00:54:38,284 --> 00:54:46,946
gestation in 25 percent of cases and development of a postpartum flare in a third (Danielsson Borssén, 2016).

420
00:54:46,896 --> 00:54:51,559
However, adverse outcomes were not greater in women with cirrhosis.

421
00:54:51,559 --> 00:55:01,021
Given the important role of procoagulant production, surveillance of coagulation indices is advised to minimize obstetrical hemorrhage complications.

422
00:55:01,021 --> 00:55:06,496
Also, the usual vaccines, along with pneumococcal vaccine, should be given .

423
00:55:06,496 --> 00:55:09,496
# Iron and Copper Overload.

424
00:55:09,496 --> 00:55:14,559
Chronic hepatitis and cirrhosis can result from iron and copper overload.

425
00:55:14,559 --> 00:55:26,809
Iron overload may stem from a primary cause, such as hereditary hemochromatosis, or from secondary complications of erythrocyte disorders (Chap. 56 p. 1052).

426
00:55:26,809 --> 00:55:35,234
Many gene mutations underlying hereditary hemochromatosis involve hepcidin and result in dysregulated iron transport.

427
00:55:35,234 --> 00:55:45,121
Some of these mutations are more common in certain populations from northern Europe (Pietrangelo, 2016; Salgia, 2015).

428
00:55:45,121 --> 00:55:52,084
Cardiomyopathy, diabetes, joint disease, and skin changes can coexist with liver disease.

429
00:55:52,084 --> 00:56:00,709
Diagnosis is made by identifying an HFE gene mutation and elevated serum ferritin and transferrin saturation levels.

430
00:56:00,709 --> 00:56:07,084
An abnormal HFE gene alone is not diagnostic because the mutation has low penetrance.

431
00:56:07,084 --> 00:56:14,309
Pregnancy outcomes are driven by the degree of liver dysfunction, although higher iron levels may affect birthweight .

432
00:56:14,309 --> 00:56:27,496
A form of neonatal hemochromatosis that does not affect the mother is now thought to be alloimmune and is called gestational alloimmune liver disease (Anastasio, 2016; Ibrahim, 2020).

433
00:56:27,496 --> 00:56:38,621
With this, maternal autoantibodies cross to the fetus and mediate dysfunction of iron homeostasis, although the antigenic target of these alloantibodies remains unclear.

434
00:56:38,621 --> 00:56:46,221
It is associated with significant neonatal morbidity and mortality and frequently recurs in subsequent pregnancies.

435
00:56:46,221 --> 00:56:58,096
In these cases, antepartum treatment with intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) may improve outcomes (Feldman, 2013; Roumiantsev, 2015).

436
00:56:58,096 --> 00:57:04,046
Wilson disease is caused by copper overload leading to chronic hepatitis and cirrhosis.

437
00:57:04,046 --> 00:57:09,921
Autosomal recessive mutations of the ATP7B gene underlie this disorder.

438
00:57:09,921 --> 00:57:16,496
This gene codes for the P-type ATPase involved in copper transport to ceruloplasmin and bile .

439
00:57:16,496 --> 00:57:26,546
This systemic condition can also manifest with cardiomyopathy, renal disease, neuropsychiatric symptoms, and certain endocrine abnormalities.

440
00:57:26,546 --> 00:57:35,484
Serum ceruloplasmin, slit-lamp examination, and 24-hour urinary copper excretion are used to identify this disease.

441
00:57:35,484 --> 00:57:45,146
A Kayser-Fleischer ring surrounding the iris is highly specific, but a suspected diagnosis generally requires subsequent genetic analysis confirmation.

442
00:57:45,146 --> 00:57:53,596
With Wilson disease, infertility may be present, but pregnancy outcomes among affected women are influenced by disease severity .

443
00:57:53,596 --> 00:58:03,634
In one multicenter study of 282 pregnancies, the miscarriage rate was 26 percent, and 6 percent of the women had worsening liver disease .

444
00:58:03,634 --> 00:58:07,521
Birth defects were not increased with chelation treatment.

445
00:58:07,521 --> 00:58:10,721
Maternal and neonatal outcomes were good.

446
00:58:10,721 --> 00:58:17,896
The American College of Gastroenterology states that few data guide which of the various chelating agents is best .

447
00:58:17,896 --> 00:58:26,609
These include penicillamine, zinc, and trientine, and any theoretical risks are outweighed by the risks of discontinuing therapy.

448
00:58:26,609 --> 00:58:33,571
The latter include not only hepatic decompensation, but also injury to the placenta and fetal liver.

449
00:58:33,571 --> 00:58:48,646
Accordingly, the American College of Gastroenterology recommends that pregnant women should continue their chelation therapy, although a dose reduction of 25 to 50 percent should be considered to promote wound healing in the event of a surgical delivery.

450
00:58:48,646 --> 00:58:52,084
# Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease.

451
00:58:52,084 --> 00:58:56,496
This is the most common chronic liver disease in the United States.

452
00:58:56,496 --> 00:59:04,509
Unsurprisingly, because it is frequently comorbid with obesity, it is demonstrable in 25 percent of American adults .

453
00:59:04,509 --> 00:59:15,471
Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is a macrovesicular fatty liver condition that resembles alcohol-induced liver injury but is seen without ethanol abuse.

454
00:59:15,471 --> 00:59:25,746
Its most severe form—nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) —is an increasingly recognized condition that may occasionally progress to hepatic cirrhosis.

455
00:59:25,900 --> 00:59:32,462
Currently, this is the third most common indication for liver transplantation in the United States .

456
00:59:32,412 --> 00:59:40,325
Obesity, type 2 diabetes, and hyperlipidemia—syndrome X—frequently coexist with NAFLD .

457
00:59:40,325 --> 00:59:50,200
The current hypothesis suggests that these conditions may interact with other unknown etiological agents to cause multiple insults that lead to hepatic injury .

458
00:59:50,200 --> 01:00:01,337
In one study of individuals with type 2 diabetes and normal liver enzyme levels, half had NAFLD, and those with NAFLD showed greater insulin resistance .

459
01:00:01,337 --> 01:00:11,312
In a study of obese adolescents undergoing bariatric surgery and intraoperative core liver biopsy, more than a third had fatty liver without hepatitis.

460
01:00:11,312 --> 01:00:15,675
An additional 20 percent had borderline or definite NASH .

461
01:00:15,675 --> 01:00:24,100
Liver damage follows a progressive continuum from NAFLD to NASH and then to hepatic fibrosis that may progress to cirrhosis .

462
01:00:24,100 --> 01:00:33,075
The disease is usually asymptomatic, and it is a frequent explanation for elevated serum transaminase levels found during routine screening.

463
01:00:33,075 --> 01:00:42,500
When other liver disease is excluded, NAFLD is the cause of elevated asymptomatic transaminase levels in up to 90 percent of cases.

464
01:00:42,500 --> 01:00:49,450
Currently, weight loss along with control of diabetes and dyslipidemia is the only recommended treatment.

465
01:00:49,450 --> 01:00:57,862
Pregnancy During the past decade, we have encountered an increasing number of obese and diabetic gravidas with fatty liver infiltration.

466
01:00:57,862 --> 01:01:06,012
These women appear to have no greater rates of adverse outcomes relative to liver involvement compared with pregnant women of similar weight.

467
01:01:06,012 --> 01:01:10,475
However, some emerging data indicate potential concerns.

468
01:01:10,475 --> 01:01:23,837
In one patient registry, risks of gestational diabetes, preeclampsia, preterm birth, and low-birthweight newborns were two- to threefold higher than in unaffected women (Hagström, 2016).

469
01:01:23,837 --> 01:01:35,112
Yarrington and colleagues (2016) reported a high rate of gestational diabetes among nonobese women who had elevated alanine transaminase levels in the first trimester.

470
01:01:35,112 --> 01:01:42,662
Somewhat related, in another study, almost a fourth of women with prior gestational diabetes had NAFLD .

471
01:01:42,662 --> 01:01:50,537
Last, some evidence suggests that the fetus of an affected mother is at increased risk for NAFLD in adulthood .

472
01:01:50,537 --> 01:01:52,812
# Cirrhosis.

473
01:01:52,812 --> 01:02:01,387
Irreversible chronic liver injury with extensive fibrosis and regenerative nodules is the final common pathway for several disorders.

474
01:02:01,387 --> 01:02:07,875
Laënnec cirrhosis from chronic alcohol exposure is the most frequent cause in the general population.

475
01:02:07,875 --> 01:02:15,175
However, in young women, most cases are caused by postnecrotic cirrhosis from chronic hepatitis B or C.

476
01:02:15,175 --> 01:02:25,562
As discussed, many cases of cryptogenic cirrhosis are now known to be caused by NAFLD (Ge, 2016; Goh, 2016).

477
01:02:25,562 --> 01:02:33,525
Clinical manifestations of cirrhosis include jaundice, edema, coagulopathy, and metabolic abnormalities.

478
01:02:33,525 --> 01:02:42,762
Of associated conditions, portal hypertension can lead to gastroesophageal varices, and splenomegaly may cause thrombocytopenia.

479
01:02:42,762 --> 01:02:47,162
Also, the incidence of venous thromboembolism is increased.

480
01:02:47,162 --> 01:02:54,387
The prognosis is poor, and 75 percent have progressive disease that leads to death in 1 to 5 years.

481
01:02:54,387 --> 01:02:59,725
The incidence in pregnancy was reported to be 1 case in 3300 births .

482
01:02:59,725 --> 01:03:11,775
Common complications include transient hepatic failure, variceal hemorrhage, preterm delivery, fetal-growth restriction, spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, and maternal death .

483
01:03:11,775 --> 01:03:17,275
Outcomes generally are poor, especially if esophageal varices coexist.

484
01:03:17,275 --> 01:03:24,100
Another potentially fatal cirrhosis complication arises from associated splenic artery aneurysms.

485
01:03:24,100 --> 01:03:31,950
Up to 20 percent of aneurysm ruptures occur during pregnancy, and 70 percent of these rupture in the third trimester .

486
01:03:31,950 --> 01:03:38,737
The 20-percent maternal mortality rate is likely related to the emergent diagnosis of these aneurysms .

487
01:03:38,737 --> 01:03:42,950
# Portal Hypertension and Esophageal Varices.

488
01:03:42,950 --> 01:03:51,675
In pregnant women, the causes of esophageal varices are equally divided between cirrhosis and extrahepatic portal vein obstruction .

489
01:03:51,675 --> 01:03:59,787
Of the extrahepatic cases, some develop following portal vein thrombosis associated with one of the thrombophilia syndromes .

490
01:03:59,787 --> 01:04:08,062
Others follow thrombosis related to umbilical vein catheterization when the woman was a neonate, especially if she was born preterm.

491
01:04:08,062 --> 01:04:10,837
Last, the rare Budd-Chiari

492
01:04:10,996 --> 01:04:17,208
syndrome results from hepatic vein thrombosis that causes extrahepatic portal hypertension.

493
01:04:17,158 --> 01:04:24,396
Of 16 pregnancies complicated by this syndrome, one observational study reported favorable outcomes .

494
01:04:24,396 --> 01:04:37,171
With either intrahepatic or extrahepatic resistance to flow, portal vein pressure rises from its normal range of 5 to 10 mm Hg, and values may exceed 30 mm Hg.

495
01:04:37,171 --> 01:04:42,858
Collateral circulation develops that carries portal blood to the systemic circulation.

496
01:04:42,858 --> 01:04:50,508
Blood drains into the gastric, intercostal, and other veins to reach the esophageal system, where varices develop.

497
01:04:50,508 --> 01:04:54,583
In rare cases, abdominal wall varices develop .

498
01:04:54,583 --> 01:05:01,133
Bleeding is usually from varices near the gastroesophageal junction, and hemorrhage can be severe.

499
01:05:01,133 --> 01:05:09,496
Bleeding during pregnancy from varices occurs in a third to half of affected women and is the major cause of maternal mortality in this group .

500
01:05:09,496 --> 01:05:15,283
Maternal prognosis with esophageal varices largely depends on whether these rupture.

501
01:05:15,283 --> 01:05:23,733
The mortality rate is 18 percent if varices are associated with cirrhosis compared with 2 percent for varices without cirrhosis.

502
01:05:23,733 --> 01:05:31,233
Similarly, perinatal mortality rates are high in women with varices and are worse if cirrhosis caused the varices.

503
01:05:31,233 --> 01:05:39,946
Increased rates of neonatal demise, preterm birth, low birthweight, preeclampsia, and postpartum hemorrhage have been reported .

504
01:05:39,946 --> 01:05:43,508
Treatment is the same as for nonpregnant patients.

505
01:05:43,508 --> 01:05:51,808
Preventively, all patients with cirrhosis, including pregnant women, should undergo endoscopic screening for variceal dilation .

506
01:05:51,808 --> 01:06:01,621
Beta-blocking drugs such as propranolol are given to decrease portal pressure and bleeding risk (Ge, 2016; Tran, 2016).

507
01:06:01,621 --> 01:06:12,496
For acute bleeding and for prophylaxis, endoscopic band ligation is preferred to sclerotherapy because it avoids any potential risk of injecting sclerotherapeutic chemicals.

508
01:06:12,496 --> 01:06:23,558
Acute medical management for bleeding varices verified endoscopically includes the intravenous vasoconstrictors octreotide or somatostatin along with endoscopic banding.

509
01:06:23,558 --> 01:06:26,508
Vasopressin is less often used.

510
01:06:26,508 --> 01:06:38,483
If endoscopy is not available, balloon tamponade using a triple-lumen Sengstaken-Blakemore tube, which is placed into the esophagus and stomach to compress bleeding varices, can be lifesaving.

511
01:06:38,483 --> 01:06:55,333
An interventional radiology procedure—transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic stent shunting (TIPSS)—also can control bleeding from gastric varices that is unresponsive to other measures (Ge, 2016; Tan, 2008).

512
01:06:55,333 --> 01:07:02,483
We have had good outcomes with this procedure done electively during pregnancy in women with prior variceal hemorrhage .

513
01:07:02,483 --> 01:07:05,971
# Acetaminophen Hepatotoxicity.

514
01:07:05,971 --> 01:07:14,358
As discussed earlier (p. 1030), acetaminophen is the most frequent cause of acute liver failure in the United States .

515
01:07:14,358 --> 01:07:23,971
This commonly used medication can be associated with hepatotoxicity at doses above 4 g/d, and even less in certain populations .

516
01:07:23,971 --> 01:07:34,721
The drug is often used during pregnancy, and overdose–either accidentally or by attempted suicide–may lead to hepatocellular necrosis and acute liver failure .

517
01:07:34,721 --> 01:07:39,658
Massive necrosis causes a cytokine storm and multiorgan dysfunction.

518
01:07:39,658 --> 01:07:45,896
Early overdose symptoms are nausea, vomiting, diaphoresis, malaise, and pallor.

519
01:07:45,896 --> 01:07:54,433
After a latent period of 24 to 48 hours, liver failure ensues, and it usually begins to resolve in 5 days.

520
01:07:54,433 --> 01:08:05,496
In a prospective Danish study, only 35 percent of patients who were treated for fulminant hepatic failure spontaneously recovered before being listed for liver transplantation .

521
01:08:05,496 --> 01:08:10,821
The antidote is N-acetylcysteine (NAC), which must be given promptly.

522
01:08:10,821 --> 01:08:19,071
The drug is thought to raise glutathione levels, which aid metabolism of the toxic metabolite, N-acetyl-p-benzoquinoneimine.

523
01:08:19,071 --> 01:08:29,008
The Rumack-Matthew nomogram uses serum acetaminophen levels to predict subsequent plasma hepatotoxic levels as a function of the time from acute ingestion .

524
01:08:29,008 --> 01:08:35,783
The need for treatment is based on these projections, and online nomograms and calculators are available.

525
01:08:35,783 --> 01:08:43,433
If the plasma level exceeds 150 μg/mL 4 hours after ingestion, treatment is given.

526
01:08:43,433 --> 01:08:52,208
When plasma determinations are not available, empirical treatment is initiated if the ingested amount is >7.5 g.

527
01:08:52,208 --> 01:08:57,246
An oral loading dose of 140 mg/kg is given

528
01:08:57,412 --> 01:09:04,599
followed by 70 mg/kg every 4 hours for a total treatment duration of 72 hours .

529
01:09:04,549 --> 01:09:12,549
Both the oral and an equally efficacious intravenous dosing regimen have been reviewed by Hodgman and Garrard (2012).

530
01:09:12,549 --> 01:09:21,399
Although the drug reaches therapeutic concentrations in the fetus, any protective effects of NAC during acetaminophen overdose are unknown .

531
01:09:21,399 --> 01:09:31,549
After 14 weeks’ gestation, the fetus has some cytochrome P450 activity necessary for metabolism of acetaminophen to the toxic metabolite.

532
01:09:31,549 --> 01:09:40,924
Riggs and associates (1989) reported follow-up data from the Rocky Mountain Poison and Drug Center in 60 women suffering overdose.

533
01:09:40,924 --> 01:09:47,312
The likelihood of maternal and fetal survival was better if the antidote was given soon after overdose.

534
01:09:47,312 --> 01:09:55,587
At least one 33-week fetus appears to have died as a direct result of hepatotoxicity 2 days after maternal ingestion.

535
01:09:55,587 --> 01:10:07,562
In another case, Wang and coworkers (1997) confirmed acetaminophen placental transfer and found maternal and cord blood levels measuring 41 μg/mL.

536
01:10:07,562 --> 01:10:12,824
Both the mother and the emergently delivered neonate died from hepatorenal failure.

537
01:10:12,824 --> 01:10:16,124
# Focal Nodular Hyperplasia.

538
01:10:16,124 --> 01:10:26,349
This benign lesion of the liver is characterized in most cases by a welldelineated accumulation of normal but disordered hepatocytes surrounding a central stellate scar.

539
01:10:26,349 --> 01:10:32,387
These usually can be differentiated from hepatic adenomas by MR and CT imaging.

540
01:10:32,387 --> 01:10:41,312
Except in rare situations of unremitting pain, surgery is rarely indicated, and most women remain asymptomatic during pregnancy.

541
01:10:41,312 --> 01:10:47,199
In one review of 20 cases, no woman had related complications during pregnancy .

542
01:10:47,199 --> 01:10:56,862
Three women showed 20percent tumor growth; in half, the tumor diminished in size; and the remaining seven tumors were unchanged across pregnancy.

543
01:10:56,862 --> 01:11:09,537
In another surveillance of 44 lesions with MR imaging in 30 gravidas, tumor size was unchanged in 80 percent and decreased in most of the remainder (Ramírez-Fuentes, 2013).

544
01:11:09,537 --> 01:11:17,962
Investigators concluded that size changes were unrelated to pregnancy, combination oral contraceptive use, or menopause.

545
01:11:17,962 --> 01:11:23,137
This lesion is not a contraindication to estrogen-containing contraceptives .

546
01:11:23,137 --> 01:11:26,237
# Hepatocellular Adenoma.

547
01:11:26,237 --> 01:11:30,737
This rare benign neoplasm develops most often in young women.

548
01:11:30,737 --> 01:11:37,987
Lesions are more common in individuals taking combination oral contraceptives or other steroid medications .

549
01:11:37,987 --> 01:11:47,724
Hepatic adenomas have a 5-percent risk of malignant transformation and a significant risk of rupture-associated hemorrhage, particularly in pregnancy.

550
01:11:47,724 --> 01:11:56,387
The rupture risk progresses with lesion size, and surgery is generally recommended for tumors measuring >5 cm .

551
01:11:56,387 --> 01:12:04,274
Thus, differentiation from focal nodular hyperplasia is important and can be achieved by MR or CT imaging.

552
01:12:04,274 --> 01:12:12,124
Tran and associates (2016) recommend sonographic surveillance of hepatic adenomas during pregnancy.

553
01:12:12,124 --> 01:12:22,612
In a study of 51 pregnancies in women with an adenoma <5 cm, 25 percent of the lesions expanded approximately 14 mm .

554
01:12:22,612 --> 01:12:30,374
In one review of 27 cases in pregnancy, 23 became apparent in the third trimester and puerperium .

555
01:12:30,374 --> 01:12:36,249
No bleeding complications followed tumors measuring <6.5 cm.

556
01:12:36,249 --> 01:12:43,674
However, 60 percent presented with tumor rupture that resulted in seven maternal deaths and six fetal deaths.

557
01:12:43,674 --> 01:12:51,337
Of note, 13 of 27 women presented within 2 months postpartum, and in half, hemorrhage heralded rupture.

558
01:12:51,337 --> 01:12:57,099
Bleeding adenomas can be managed by angiographic embolization followed by resection .

559
01:12:57,099 --> 01:12:59,987
# Liver Transplantation.

560
01:12:59,987 --> 01:13:11,212
According to the Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network (2020) more than 177,000 liver transplantations have been performed in the United States.

561
01:13:11,212 --> 01:13:18,899
Analysis of the National Inpatient Sample database found 2 liver transplantations per 100,000 deliveries .

562
01:13:18,899 --> 01:13:25,562
In most studies, the 80-percent live-birth rate compares favorably with that of the general population.

563
01:13:25,562 --> 01:13:40,537
However, risks of preeclampsia, cesarean delivery, fetal-growth restriction, and preterm birth are significantly elevated (Coscia, 2010; Deshpande, 2012; Kanzaki, 2016).

564
01:13:40,537 --> 01:13:46,299
Although cesarean delivery is common, vaginal delivery is not contraindicated .

565
01:13:46,299 --> 01:13:55,237
Mattila and colleagues (2017) found that half of the women they cared for had maternal complications, including acute graft

566
01:13:55,396 --> 01:13:57,083
rejection.

567
01:13:57,033 --> 01:14:06,658
Importantly, 4 percent of mothers had died within a year after delivery, but this rate is comparable to that in nonpregnant liver transplantation patients.

568
01:14:06,658 --> 01:14:08,896
Gallbladder Disorders.

569
01:14:08,896 --> 01:14:12,646
# Cholelithiasis and Cholecystitis.

570
01:14:12,646 --> 01:14:17,271
In the United States, 17 percent of women have gallstones.

571
01:14:17,271 --> 01:14:24,933
Most stones contain cholesterol, and its oversecretion into bile is thought to be a major factor in stone formation.

572
01:14:24,933 --> 01:14:30,871
The cumulative risk of all patients with gallstones to become symptomatic is 20 percent .

573
01:14:30,871 --> 01:14:37,283
For this reason, prophylactic cholecystectomy is not warranted for asymptomatic stones.

574
01:14:37,283 --> 01:14:47,358
Symptomatic cholelithiasis typically presents with right upper or epigastric abdominal pain, bloating, belching, nausea, and fatty food intolerance.

575
01:14:47,358 --> 01:14:53,083
Pain stems from gallbladder contraction, which forces a stone up into the sac’s neck.

576
01:14:53,083 --> 01:14:58,971
With subsequent gallbladder relaxation, the stone falls back to relieve the obstruction.

577
01:14:58,971 --> 01:15:08,871
Laboratory values are normal, and leukocytosis or elevated liver and pancreatic enzymes should warn for acute cholecystitis or pancreatitis.

578
01:15:08,871 --> 01:15:14,396
Standard care for symptomatic cholelithiasis is laparoscopic cholecystectomy .

579
01:15:14,396 --> 01:15:24,546
Less often, oral ursodeoxycholic acid therapy or extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy are used, but experience with these during pregnancy is lacking.

580
01:15:24,546 --> 01:15:32,596
Acute cholecystitis usually develops when a stone obstructs the cystic duct, which connects the gallbladder to the common bile duct.

581
01:15:32,596 --> 01:15:37,733
Bacterial infection plays a role in 50 to 85 percent of cases.

582
01:15:37,733 --> 01:15:46,133
In more than half of patients with acute cholecystitis, a history of prior right upper quadrant pain from biliary colic is elicited.

583
01:15:46,133 --> 01:15:54,596
With acute disease, pain is accompanied by anorexia, nausea and vomiting, low-grade fever, and mild leukocytosis.

584
01:15:54,596 --> 01:16:04,121
As shown in Figure 58-3, sonography will display stones, and both falsepositive and false-negative rates range from 2 to 4 percent .

585
01:16:04,121 --> 01:16:14,008
In acute cases, medical therapy consists of intravenous fluids, antibiotics, analgesics, and in some instances, nasogastric suction.

586
01:16:14,008 --> 01:16:20,308
Surgical therapy follows, and laparoscopic cholecystectomy is the preferred route for most.

587
01:16:20,308 --> 01:16:22,858
Figure 58-3.

588
01:16:22,858 --> 01:16:30,183
Sonogram shows multiple, hyperechoic gallstones collecting along the inferior wall of an anechoic gallbladder.

589
01:16:30,183 --> 01:16:33,583
# Gallbladder Disease During Pregnancy.

590
01:16:33,583 --> 01:16:41,396
After the first trimester, the gallbladder fasting volume and the residual volume after postprandial emptying are doubled.

591
01:16:41,396 --> 01:16:48,833
Incomplete emptying may result in retention of cholesterol crystals, a prerequisite for cholesterol gallstones.

592
01:16:48,833 --> 01:16:58,008
From studies, the combined incidence of biliary sludge and gallstones is 5 to 8 percent (Ko, 2005, 2014).

593
01:16:58,008 --> 01:17:03,746
Postpartum, sludge frequently regresses, and occasionally gallstones will resorb.

594
01:17:03,746 --> 01:17:11,196
Still, gallbladder diseases are the most common cause of nonobstetrical admissions in the first year following delivery .

595
01:17:11,196 --> 01:17:16,271
This is particularly true for women managed conservatively during pregnancy.

596
01:17:16,271 --> 01:17:27,033
Jorge and coworkers (2015) reported that half of 53 women with symptomatic cholelithiasis in pregnancy underwent later postpartum cholecystectomy.

597
01:17:27,033 --> 01:17:32,408
In 80 percent of these women, recurrent symptoms had developed prior to surgery.

598
01:17:32,408 --> 01:17:35,883
# Medical versus Surgical Management.

599
01:17:35,883 --> 01:17:39,833
Symptomatic cholelithiasis is common in pregnancy.

600
01:17:39,833 --> 01:17:52,758
Othman and colleagues (2012) showed that gravidas managed conservatively had greater pain, more recurrent emergency department visits, more hospitalizations, and higher cesarean delivery rate.

601
01:17:52,758 --> 01:18:03,608
Dhupar and associates (2010) reported more complications with conservative management of gallbladder disease compared with laparoscopic cholecystectomy in pregnancy.

602
01:18:03,608 --> 01:18:13,371
These included multiple admissions, prolonged total parenteral nutrition (TPN), and unplanned labor induction for worsening gallbladder symptoms.

603
01:18:13,371 --> 01:18:20,058
Therefore, operative and endoscopic interventions are increasingly favored over conservative measures.

604
01:18:20,058 --> 01:18:25,196
Acute cholecystitis during pregnancy or the puerperium is also common.

605
01:18:25,196 --> 01:18:33,483
Acute disease in pregnancy may be complicated by sepsis, pancreatitis, venous thromboembolism, and bowel obstruction .

606
01:18:33,483 --> 01:18:38,608
Cholecystitis is initially managed similarly to that for nonpregnant women.

607
01:18:38,608 --> 01:18:41,633
In the distant past, most favored

608
01:18:41,788 --> 01:18:43,788
medical therapy.

609
01:18:43,738 --> 01:18:54,200
However, the recurrence rate during the same pregnancy is high, and 25 to 50 percent of women ultimately require cholecystectomy for persistent symptoms .

610
01:18:54,200 --> 01:19:03,300
Moreover, if cholecystitis recurs later in gestation, preterm labor is more likely and cholecystectomy is technically more difficult.

611
01:19:03,300 --> 01:19:07,838
Cholecystectomy can be performed safely in all trimesters .

612
01:19:07,838 --> 01:19:15,563
A metaanalysis found that cholecystectomy does not raise the risk of preterm labor or of maternal or fetal mortality .

613
01:19:15,563 --> 01:19:25,425
Of surgical routes, laparoscopic cholecystectomy has evolved as the favored approach (Pearl, 2017; Shigemi, 2019).

614
01:19:25,425 --> 01:19:30,500
This is discussed further in Chapter 49 (p. 867).

615
01:19:30,500 --> 01:19:39,888
Management at Parkland Hospital favors a surgical approach, especially if biliary pancreatitis, as subsequently discussed, is comorbid .

616
01:19:39,888 --> 01:19:44,388
# Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography.

617
01:19:44,388 --> 01:19:50,388
Approximately 10 percent of patients with symptomatic stone disease have common duct stones .

618
01:19:50,388 --> 01:20:03,788
Symptomatic biliary duct gallstones during pregnancy can be retrieved by endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) (Fogel, 2014; Greenberger, 2018).

619
01:20:03,788 --> 01:20:08,838
The procedure is performed if common duct obstruction is suspected or proven.

620
01:20:08,838 --> 01:20:15,350
ERCP can be modified in many cases so that radiation exposure from fluoroscopy is avoided .

621
01:20:15,350 --> 01:20:22,313
If standard fluoroscopy is used, a lead apron shield is placed between the radiation source and the fetus.

622
01:20:22,313 --> 01:20:34,163
In 68 ERCP procedures performed in pregnant women at Parkland Hospital, all but two women had gallstones, and common duct stones were identified in half of 65 women .

623
01:20:34,163 --> 01:20:38,050
Stones were successfully removed in all but one woman.

624
01:20:38,050 --> 01:20:43,725
A biliary stent was placed in 22 percent of cases and removed after delivery.

625
01:20:43,725 --> 01:20:50,088
Complications were minimal, but postERCP pancreatitis developed in 16 percent.

626
01:20:50,088 --> 01:20:55,550
Pregnancy outcomes were not different from those in the general obstetrical population.

627
01:20:55,550 --> 01:21:03,125
As a less invasive approach, MR cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) in pregnancy has been reported .

628
01:21:03,125 --> 01:21:07,750
Ascending cholangitis can complicate acute biliary obstruction.

629
01:21:07,750 --> 01:21:15,313
Nearly 70 percent of affected patients develop Charcot triad—jaundice, abdominal pain, and fever.

630
01:21:15,313 --> 01:21:23,500
The diagnosis is aided by sonography, and treatment is broad-spectrum antibiotics and biliary drainage by ERCP .

631
01:21:23,500 --> 01:21:25,875
Pancreatic Disorders.

632
01:21:25,875 --> 01:21:28,488
# Pancreatitis.

633
01:21:28,488 --> 01:21:36,738
Acute pancreatic inflammation is triggered by factors that cause activation of pancreatic trypsinogen followed by autodigestion.

634
01:21:36,738 --> 01:21:43,788
It is characterized by cellmembrane disruption and proteolysis, edema, hemorrhage, and necrosis .

635
01:21:43,788 --> 01:21:51,450
Up to 10 percent of patients develop necrotizing pancreatitis, which carries a mortality risk of 15 percent.

636
01:21:51,450 --> 01:21:54,700
This rate rises if infection develops .

637
01:21:54,700 --> 01:21:59,313
The incidence of pancreatitis varies with the population studied.

638
01:21:59,313 --> 01:22:09,325
At Parkland Hospital, with a predominant Mexican-American population, acute pancreatitis complicated approximately 1 in 3300 pregnancies .

639
01:22:09,325 --> 01:22:20,988
From other reviews, the incidence is 1 case in 3500 to 6000 pregnancies (Eddy, 2008; Hacker, 2015; Hernandez, 2007).

640
01:22:20,988 --> 01:22:28,475
In nonpregnant patients, acute pancreatitis is almost equally associated with gallstones and alcohol abuse.

641
01:22:28,475 --> 01:22:34,800
During pregnancy, however, cholelithiasis is almost always the predisposing condition.

642
01:22:34,800 --> 01:22:52,388
Other causes are hyperlipidemias, usually hypertriglyceridemia; hyperparathyroidism; congenital ductal anomalies; recent ERCP; some drugs; and rarely autoimmune pancreatitis (Conwell, 2018; Tang, 2018).

643
01:22:52,388 --> 01:23:01,800
Nonbiliary pancreatitis occasionally develops postoperatively, or it is associated with trauma, drugs, or some viral infections.

644
01:23:01,800 --> 01:23:11,050
Certain metabolic conditions, including acute AFLP and familial hypertriglyceridemia, also predispose to pancreatitis .

645
01:23:11,050 --> 01:23:20,025
Cases of acute and chronic pancreatitis have been linked to numerous mutations of the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator gene .

646
01:23:20,025 --> 01:23:28,725
Diagnosis Acute pancreatitis incites incapacitating epigastric pain, nausea and vomiting, and abdominal distention.

647
01:23:28,725 --> 01:23:36,150
Patients are usually distressed and have low-grade fever, tachycardia, hypotension, and abdominal tenderness.

648
01:23:36,150 --> 01:23:44,150
As many as 10 percent have sepsis, which causes endothelial activation and can lead to acute respiratory distress syndrome .

649
01:23:44,150 --> 01:23:45,663
Serum

650
01:23:45,820 --> 01:23:52,395
lipase measurements are preferred for diagnosis, however, amylase levels also can be used.

651
01:23:52,345 --> 01:24:04,870
In 173 pregnant women with pancreatitis, the mean amylase value approximated 2000 IU/L, and the mean lipase value approached 3000 IU/L .

652
01:24:04,870 --> 01:24:11,157
Importantly, the degree of enzyme elevation and disease severity do not reliably correlate.

653
01:24:11,157 --> 01:24:19,307
By 48 to 72 hours amylase levels may return to normal despite other evidence for continuing pancreatitis.

654
01:24:19,307 --> 01:24:24,770
Serum lipase activity typically remains increased with continued inflammation.

655
01:24:24,770 --> 01:24:30,945
Leukocytosis is usually found, and 25 percent of patients have hypocalcemia.

656
01:24:30,945 --> 01:24:37,982
Elevated serum bilirubin and aspartate transaminase levels may signify concomitant gallstone disease.

657
01:24:37,982 --> 01:24:40,645
Table 58-5.

658
01:24:40,645 --> 01:24:50,920
Laboratory Values in 173 Pregnant Women with Acute Pancreatitis Several prognostic scoring systems are used to classify pancreatitis severity.

659
01:24:50,920 --> 01:24:57,807
However, the Ranson criteria and the Apache II scoring system may be less relevant for pregnancy.

660
01:24:57,807 --> 01:25:10,995
In contrast, the Atlanta Classification incorporates the degree of organ failure as a measure of severity and may be more applicable in pregnancy (Cain, 2015; Conwell, 2018).

661
01:25:10,995 --> 01:25:16,882
With this last tool, mild disease lacks organ failure or systemic complications.

662
01:25:16,882 --> 01:25:25,382
Moderately severe disease shows organ failure lasting <48 h, with or without local or systemic complications.

663
01:25:25,382 --> 01:25:30,270
Severe disease is defined by persistent single- or multi-organ failure .

664
01:25:30,270 --> 01:25:34,745
Management Medical treatment mirrors that for nonpregnant patients.

665
01:25:34,745 --> 01:25:43,357
This includes analgesics, intravenous hydration, and measures to decrease pancreatic secretion by interdiction of oral intake.

666
01:25:43,357 --> 01:25:48,595
Nasogastric suction does not improve outcomes of mild to moderate disease.

667
01:25:48,595 --> 01:26:00,007
In a series by Ramin and colleagues (1995), all 43 affected pregnant women responded to conservative treatment and were hospitalized for a mean of 8.5 days.

668
01:26:00,007 --> 01:26:10,107
If bacterial superinfection, necrotizing pancreatitis, sepsis, or cholangitis is found, broad-spectrum antimicrobials are administered.

669
01:26:10,107 --> 01:26:14,445
If common duct stones are found, ERCP is indicated.

670
01:26:14,445 --> 01:26:20,032
Enteral feeding may be helpful once pain improves and associated ileus resolves.

671
01:26:20,032 --> 01:26:33,482
For women with more severe pancreatitis and prolonged disease course, total enteral nutrition using nasojejunal feeding is superior to TPN (Cain, 2015; Conwell, 2018).

672
01:26:33,482 --> 01:26:42,770
Cholecystectomy is considered after inflammation subsides because women with gallstone pancreatitis carry a greater risk of recurrent inflammation .

673
01:26:42,770 --> 01:26:55,257
Juo and coworkers (2018) reported that a third of women with biliary pancreatitis not treated with cholecystectomy were readmitted, and 30 percent of these had recurrent pancreatitis.

674
01:26:55,257 --> 01:27:02,332
Of the initial group, those who instead did undergo cholecystectomy had only a 5-percent readmission rate.

675
01:27:02,332 --> 01:27:09,757
Pregnancy Outcomes Increasing severity of pancreatitis is associated with adverse maternal and fetal outcomes .

676
01:27:09,757 --> 01:27:22,720
In one review of 101 pancreatitis cases, Eddy and coworkers (2008) found a 30-percent preterm delivery rate, and 11 percent were delivered before 35 weeks’ gestation.

677
01:27:22,720 --> 01:27:25,770
There were also 4 percent stillbirths.

678
01:27:25,770 --> 01:27:29,557
There were two pancreatitis-related maternal deaths.

679
01:27:29,557 --> 01:27:35,157
Importantly, almost a third of women had recurrent pancreatitis during pregnancy.

680
01:27:35,157 --> 01:27:45,557
In another study of 342 pregnancies complicated by pancreatitis, preterm delivery and fetal mortality rates were comparable to the data from Eddy .

681
01:27:45,557 --> 01:27:48,795
# Pancreatic Transplantation.

682
01:27:48,795 --> 01:27:53,707
Few reports describe pregnancy following pancreas transplantation.

683
01:27:53,707 --> 01:28:03,670
Of 44 pregnancies in 73 women following pancreas-kidney transplantation, outcomes are encouraging, and vaginal delivery has been described .

684
01:28:03,670 --> 01:28:13,182
Although the incidence of hypertension, preeclampsia, preterm delivery, and fetal-growth restriction are high, there was only one perinatal death.

685
01:28:13,182 --> 01:28:18,320
Four rejection episodes developed during pregnancy and were treated successfully.

686
01:28:18,320 --> 01:28:25,282
Pancreatic islet autotransplantation and at least three subsequent successful pregnancies have been reported .

